Subject: Re: [PATCH] board: microchip: mpfs_generic: Allow serial number read failures
In-Reply-To: <20261005004902.3441895-1-bin.meng@processmission.com>
References: <20261005004902.3441895-1-bin.meng@processmission.com>
Date: Mon, 05 Oct 2026 23:59:00 +0000

Thanks for the patch. Comments on the actual diff:

> +static bool mac_addr_valid;
...
> -		printf("Cannot read device serial number\n");
> -		return -EINVAL;
> +		printf("Warning: Cannot read device serial number\n");
> +		goto out;

1. Logging: the message is still printed, now prefixed with "Warning:"
   instead of returning an error, which is a reasonable way to signal
   "this is now non-fatal but still worth telling the user about". One
   nit: this uses printf() at default level while most other failure
   paths in board_late_init() in this file appear to just printf() too
   (not log_err/log_warning), so this is at least consistent with the
   existing style in this function, even if it's not using the
   log_* macros framework.

2. Fallback/default serial: no fallback serial number is substituted
   into mac_addr on failure -- mac_addr stays whatever it was
   zero-initialized to (static array, so all-zero). That's fine
   *because* of the new mac_addr_valid flag; the all-zero mac_addr is
   never actually applied to the device tree since:

   > +	if (!mac_addr_valid)
   > +		return 0;

   in ft_board_setup() short-circuits before the fdt_setprop() call
   that would otherwise write the (invalid/zero) mac_addr into
   "local-mac-address". This correctly prevents propagating a garbage
   all-zero MAC to the DT. Good catch handling that side effect of the
   early-exit.

3. Control flow / "goto out": the new `out:` label falls through to
   `mpfs_syscontroller_process_dtbo(sys_serv_priv);` before `return 0;`
   -- so DT overlay processing still happens on the failure path, which
   matches the stated intent ("skip the MAC updates while still
   processing device tree overlays"). Confirmed this is what the diff
   actually does: the `mac_addr_valid = true;` assignment sits right
   before the `out:` label, so it's skipped by the goto but the shared
   tail (dtbo processing + return 0) still executes. That looks
   correct.

4. Return code consistency: board_late_init() now *always* returns 0
   on this path (both success and serial-read-failure cases), whereas
   previously it returned -EINVAL on failure. Is that an intentional
   part of "should not halt boot", or could callers of
   board_late_init() that check the return value for other error
   conditions be affected? Worth a one-line note in the commit message
   confirming board_late_init()'s return value isn't checked
   meaningfully elsewhere for this board, since the error swallowing
   is now total (function always succeeds past this point).

5. Minor: mac_addr_valid is function-static-file-scope and
   implicitly initialized to false, which is correct for the "treat
   unset as invalid" default register, consistent with mac_addr's
   existing zero-init pattern in the same file.

No Reviewed-by or other review tags have been applied to this patch
yet.

Verdict: Logic is sound and the mac_addr_valid guard correctly
prevents writing a bogus zeroed MAC into the FDT on failure; would
like the commit message to clarify point 4 (whether always-returning-0
from board_late_init() has any downstream impact) before this is
ready to pick up.
